<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A boxed mesh pattern is a mesh pattern whose selected entries lie in an empty
axis-parallel rectangle. We classify coincidences of boxed patterns with classical and vincular
patterns, exhibit a genuinely bivincular coincidence, and prove that no
boxed–bivincular coincidence occurs for patterns of length at least five.
Together with known results, this shows that every boxed pattern of length at
least five has factorial growth and hence fails the Stanley–Wilf property.
At length four, one exceptional orbit is enumerated by the semi-Baxter
numbers, while the remaining exceptional orbit, <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2143,3412\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2143</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3412</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2143,3412\}</annotation></semantics></math>, is
unresolved; we conjecture that it has factorial growth.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(123)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(123)</annotation></semantics></math>, we derive an exact maximum-insertion identity and prove the
subfactorial upper bound <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{5n}n^{\beta n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{5n}n^{\beta n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\log_{2}(2\cos(\pi/7))&lt;0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\log_{2}(2\cos(\pi/7))&lt;0.85</annotation></semantics></math>. A closed enumeration remains open. We also prove a general first-moment
formula for boxed mesh patterns that depends only on the length of the
underlying pattern; in particular, the expected number of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(123)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(123)</annotation></semantics></math>
occurrences in a uniformly random permutation of length <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotic to
<math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\log n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">For <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(12)</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify the occurrence statistic with the up-degree in
the strong Bruhat order, obtaining its maximum, its mean, and an exact
insertion identity for the distribution polynomials. We conjecture that the
coefficients of these polynomials are unimodal.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords.</span> mesh pattern; boxed pattern; Stanley–Wilf property;
vincular pattern; bivincular pattern; semi-Baxter permutation;
strong Bruhat order.</p>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2020 Mathematics Subject Classification.</span> 05A05, 05A15, 05A16</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of permutations of
<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]=\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]=\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{0}=\{\varepsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{0}=\{\varepsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A
<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">classical pattern</em> of length <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation
<math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p=p_{1}p_{2}\cdots p_{k}\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=p_{1}p_{2}\cdots p_{k}\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. An occurrence of <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in a
permutation <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi=\pi_{1}\pi_{2}\cdots\pi_{n}\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\pi_{1}\pi_{2}\cdots\pi_{n}\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subsequence
<math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{i_{1}}\pi_{i_{2}}\cdots\pi_{i_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i_{1}}\pi_{i_{2}}\cdots\pi_{i_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}&lt;i_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}&lt;i_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is order-isomorphic to <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i_{r}}&lt;\pi_{i_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i_{r}}&lt;\pi_{i_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}&lt;p_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}&lt;p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r,s\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r,s\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, the pattern <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="132" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>132</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">132</annotation></semantics></math> occurs twice in the permutation <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24513" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24513</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24513</annotation></semantics></math>,
namely as the subsequences <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="243" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>243</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">243</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="253" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>253</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">253</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a pattern <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, defined in any specified sense, let
<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)\subseteq\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)\subseteq\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of permutations that
avoid <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, contain no occurrence of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Stanley–Wilf limit</em> of a pattern <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, when it exists, is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(p)=\lim_{n\to\infty}|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|^{1/n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(p)=\lim_{n\to\infty}|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|^{1/n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Arratia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> proved that an exponential upper bound for
<math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|</annotation></semantics></math> for a classical pattern <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> implies the existence of this limit, and Marcus and Tardos
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> proved the required upper bound. Thus the
Stanley–Wilf theorem says that <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(p)</annotation></semantics></math> exists and is finite for every fixed
classical pattern <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. For any pattern <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, not necessarily classical, we shall say that the
<em id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Stanley–Wilf property</em> holds if</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\limsup_{n\to\infty}|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|^{1/n}&lt;\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{n\to\infty}|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|^{1/n}&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We say that a positive sequence <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> has <em id="S1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">factorial growth</em> if <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log a_{n}=\Theta(n\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log a_{n}=\Theta(n\log n)</annotation></semantics></math>,
or, equivalently, if <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}=n^{\Theta(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}=n^{\Theta(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a consecutive pattern of length at least three, meaning that the
entries in an occurrence must occupy consecutive positions, Elizalde
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite> proved that there exist constants
<math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c_{p}&lt;d_{p}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c_{p}&lt;d_{p}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for all sufficiently large <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{p}^{n}n!&lt;|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|&lt;d_{p}^{n}n!." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{p}^{n}n!&lt;|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(p)|&lt;d_{p}^{n}n!.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Consequently its <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th root tends to infinity. (Consecutive patterns of
length two are the exceptional trivial cases, with one avoider.) Vincular patterns, defined in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, already exhibit
both types of behaviour in length three: for example,
<math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\underline{23}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>23</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\underline{23}</annotation></semantics></math>, also written <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{-}23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{-}23</annotation></semantics></math>, has the Bell numbers as its
avoidance sequence, whereas some other one-bond patterns have Catalan
avoidance sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Since
<math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}^{1/n}\sim n/(e\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}^{1/n}\sim n/(e\log n)</annotation></semantics></math>, Bell growth also violates the Stanley–Wilf
property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Mesh patterns, defined in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, were introduced by Brändén and Claesson
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> as a flexible language for imposing geometric
conditions on occurrences of classical permutation patterns. They have since
developed in several complementary directions: shading and coincidence
criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, systematic and
algorithmic classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, exhaustive
generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, asymptotic containment
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and the study of distributions
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Two patterns are called <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coincident</em> when they impose exactly the same
avoidance condition: every permutation avoids one if and only if it avoids the
other. Thus coincidence identifies the actual avoidance classes, not merely
their counting sequences; a formal definition is given in
<a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Avgustinovich, Kitaev and Valyuzhenich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> introduced <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">boxed
patterns</em>: all <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> internal cells of a mesh pattern of length <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are
shaded. They showed, among other things, that the permutations avoiding boxed <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="132" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>132</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">132</annotation></semantics></math>
are exactly those avoiding classical <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="132" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>132</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">132</annotation></semantics></math> and are therefore counted by the
Catalan numbers. They also
showed that an analogue of the Erdős–Szekeres theorem fails for boxed
patterns of length greater than two, and they enumerated permutations
avoiding two or more boxed patterns of length three, where generalized
Catalan numbers appear. More strikingly,
they proved factorial lower bounds for many boxed patterns, showing that the
Stanley–Wilf conclusion for classical patterns does not extend to arbitrary
mesh patterns. The simplest of their constructions doubles each letter:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta(\pi_{1}\cdots\pi_{m})=(2\pi_{1})(2\pi_{1}-1)\cdots(2\pi_{m})(2\pi_{m}-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\pi_{1}\cdots\pi_{m})=(2\pi_{1})(2\pi_{1}-1)\cdots(2\pi_{m})(2\pi_{m}-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Every pair is a descending pair of consecutive values, and the empty-rectangle
condition shows that <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> avoids <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(123)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(123)</annotation></semantics></math>: the unselected sibling
of the middle member of any increasing triple lies inside its rectangle. This
injects <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}_{2m}(\operatorname{Box}(123))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}_{2m}(\operatorname{Box}(123))</annotation></semantics></math>. The same observation
and its complement handle any boxed pattern having a monotone interval factor
of length three. More generally, factorial growth is inherited from any boxed
interval factor; see Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3 Boxed patterns as interval-consecutive patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Their general proof calls <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau=\tau_{1}\cdots\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\tau_{1}\cdots\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">good</em> when some
<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;i&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;i&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;\tau_{i}&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;\tau_{i}&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math> and the factor
<math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i-1}\tau_{i}\tau_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i-1}\tau_{i}\tau_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonmonotone. In the representative <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="213" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>213</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">213</annotation></semantics></math>
case, every letter of an arbitrary base permutation is replaced by an
increasing pair of consecutive values. Whichever sibling plays <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, its
mate either lies in the forbidden rectangle or forces the adjacent member of
the pattern to be the mate, contradicting the required order. Reverse and
complement deal with the other three nonmonotone triples. If <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is not
good, its entries split into monotone blocks around its minimum and maximum.
Either it has a monotone factor of length three, in which case the
<math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(123)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(123)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(321)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>321</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(321)</annotation></semantics></math> construction applies, or it is one of the four
exceptional permutations below. Together these ideas give factorial lower
bounds for twenty of the twenty-four underlying permutations of length four.
Their argument left</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2143,\qquad 3412,\qquad 2413,\qquad 3142," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2143</mn><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mn>3412</mn><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mn>3142</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2143,\qquad 3412,\qquad 2413,\qquad 3142,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">and treated them as a single symmetry class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">It is important that the same theorem already settles every larger length.
More precisely, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Theorem 3]</cite> proves</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))|\geq\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor!" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))|\geq\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor!</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, other than the four permutations in
(<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Consequently every boxed pattern of length at least five
fails the Stanley–Wilf property. Thus the obstruction to coincidence with a
classical, vincular or bivincular pattern in lengths at least five is
accompanied by a much stronger growth statement, but the two facts have
logically independent proofs: noncoincidence alone would not imply factorial
growth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Three questions from the final section of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> are directly relevant
here: determine the Stanley–Wilf behaviour of the four patterns in
(<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); enumerate <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(123)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(123)</annotation></semantics></math>; and decide when a boxed pattern can be
coincident with a classical pattern. The last question has since been settled:
coincident mesh patterns have the same underlying permutation
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Lemma 4.1]</cite>, and the enclosed-diagonal
criterion of Tenner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> classifies when a mesh is superfluous.
For boxed patterns the complete list is recalled in
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. The first two questions remain the main
enumerative motivation for this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Boxed patterns are also called <em id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frame patterns</em>; under that name their
occurrence statistics have been studied in the cycle structure of
permutations by Jones, Kitaev and Remmel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. On
the algorithmic side, deciding boxed containment has been studied by Bruner
and Lackner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, by Cho, Na and Sim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>
and by Amit, Bille, Hagge Cording, Li Gørtz and Wedel Vildhøj
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>; see Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Remark 3.3. ‣ 3 Boxed patterns as interval-consecutive patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The four exceptional cases separate in two ways. First, the four patterns in
(<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are treated together in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, p. 48]</cite>, but they do
not form a single symmetry class: reverse, complement and inverse split
(<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) into</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2143,3412\}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\{2413,3142\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2143</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3412</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2413</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3142</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2143,3412\}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\{2413,3142\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">The two avoidance sequences are different: at size five they have respectively
<math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="106" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>106</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">106</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="104" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>104</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">104</annotation></semantics></math> elements. Second, the latter orbit is not a counterexample to
the Stanley–Wilf property. The key observation for this orbit, that we prove in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, is the
coincidence</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(2413)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{41}3," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(2413)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{41}3,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.3" class="ltx_p">where the underline requires the entries playing <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to be adjacent;
the equivalent dash notation is <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.
The right-hand class consists of the semi-Baxter permutations, completely
enumerated by Bouvel, Guerrini, Rechnitzer and Rinaldi
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. This settles one of the two orbits in
(<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and explains the value <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="104" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>104</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">104</annotation></semantics></math> at size five. We also classify
all coincidences of a boxed pattern with a nonclassical vincular pattern: none
exist in lengths five or more. Moreover, <math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(2413)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(2413)</annotation></semantics></math> is coincident with a
genuinely bivincular pattern, and we prove that no boxed pattern of length at
least five is coincident with any bivincular pattern.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">For the unresolved orbit <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2143,3412\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2143</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3412</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2143,3412\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we show that replacing every entry
<math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by the increasing pair <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2x-1)(2x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2x-1)(2x)</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the number of boxed <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2143" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2143</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2143</annotation></semantics></math>
occurrences. We also conjecture that the avoidance sequence associated with
this orbit has factorial growth; see Conjectures <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 7.4. ‣ 7 The remaining orbit {2143,3412} ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>
and <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem5" title="Conjecture 7.5. ‣ 7 The remaining orbit {2143,3412} ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a> and the accompanying discussion in
Section <a href="#S7" title="7 The remaining orbit {2143,3412} ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Our second focus is boxed <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="123" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>123</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">123</annotation></semantics></math>. The sequence is recorded as OEIS A201168
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, but no generating function or coefficient formula is given
there or, to our knowledge, elsewhere in the literature. We give an exact
maximum-insertion identity and show that its prefix condition is itself
enumerated by <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t-1)!</annotation></semantics></math>; this gives the five new terms <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{13},\ldots,a_{17}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>17</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{13},\ldots,a_{17}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A weighted halving argument also gives</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(123))|\leq 2^{5n}n^{\beta n},\qquad\beta=\log_{2}\!\left(2\cos\frac{\pi}{7}\right)&lt;0.85," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>7</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.85</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(123))|\leq 2^{5n}n^{\beta n},\qquad\beta=\log_{2}\!\left(2\cos\frac{\pi}{7}\right)&lt;0.85,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">so the avoidance class is subfactorial even though it does not have the
Stanley–Wilf property. The identity does not close in the total numbers,
and a closed enumeration remains open. On the distributional side, we prove
that the expected number of occurrences of <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in a uniformly random
permutation depends only on the length of <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, not on <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> itself. For
boxed <math id="S1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="123" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>123</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">123</annotation></semantics></math> the exact mean is <math id="S1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+2)H_{n}-3n\sim n\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+2)H_{n}-3n\sim n\log n</annotation></semantics></math>, so a random
permutation contains on average on the order of <math id="S1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\log n</annotation></semantics></math> such occurrences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">A complementary distribution question concerns the boxed pattern <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>. A
recent sequence of papers has completed the classification of length-two mesh
patterns by distribution-equivalence and Wilf-equivalence. Su, Kitaev and
Zhang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> obtained a near-classification; Fang, Fu,
Kitaev, Li, Su and Sun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> reduced both classifications to a
single remaining case; and Zhang and Zhao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> settled that
case. The first of these papers records the initial distribution polynomials
for boxed <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> but leaves a general formula open. We observe that the number of
boxed <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> occurrences is exactly the up-degree of a permutation in the strong
Bruhat order. The standard Bruhat cover criterion, also discussed in
mesh-pattern language by Bouvel, Ferrari and Tenner
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, therefore brings the results of Adin and
Roichman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> into the problem. In particular, it gives
the maximum number of occurrences, the number of maximizers, and the exact
mean. We add a short direct proof of the mean and an insertion identity that
isolates the catalytic prefix statistic needed for the full distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we
introduce the notation and recall the preliminary results used throughout the
paper. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Boxed patterns as interval-consecutive patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we translate boxed containment into
consecutive containment after restriction to an interval of values. In
Section <a href="#S4" title="4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we correct the length-four symmetry reduction,
identify the semi-Baxter orbit, and record the resulting global growth
classification. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we classify classical and
vincular coincidences, exhibit a genuinely bivincular coincidence, and prove
that no boxed–bivincular coincidence occurs in length at least five. In
Section <a href="#S6" title="6 The boxed mesh pattern 123 ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we give an exact identity, a subfactorial upper bound,
and a general first-moment formula motivated by boxed <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="123" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>123</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">123</annotation></semantics></math>. In
Section <a href="#S7" title="7 The remaining orbit {2143,3412} ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we discuss the remaining orbit and state two
conjectures. Finally, in Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Boxed 12 and strong Bruhat covers ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we connect boxed <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> with
covers in the strong Bruhat order and record the resulting distributional
information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w=w_{1}\cdots w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=w_{1}\cdots w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a word with distinct letters, its
<em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">standardization</em>, also called its <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reduced form</em>, is the unique
permutation <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{st}(w)\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>st</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{st}(w)\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> whose entries have the same relative
order as those of <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that a permutation <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contains</em> the
classical pattern <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{st}(\pi_{i_{1}}\cdots\pi_{i_{k}})=\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>st</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{st}(\pi_{i_{1}}\cdots\pi_{i_{k}})=\tau</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> avoids <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mesh pattern</em> of length <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(\tau,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(\tau,R)</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subseteq\{0,1,\ldots,k\}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subseteq\{0,1,\ldots,k\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its diagram consists of the plot of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, with points
<math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,\tau_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,\tau_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, together with a shaded unit square <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,a+1]\times[b,b+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,a+1]\times[b,b+1]</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\in R</annotation></semantics></math>. For example,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\bigl(2413,\{(0,1),(2,2),(3,0)\}\bigr)=\raisebox{2.58334pt}{\hbox to26.19pt{\vbox to26.19pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 0.14825pt\lower-0.14825pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}
{}{{}}{}
