<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We use the notation <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(123)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(123)</annotation></semantics></math> throughout, as it is more convenient in longer formulas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For a permutation <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi=\pi_{1}\cdots\pi_{n}\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\pi_{1}\cdots\pi_{n}\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, its <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reverse</em>,
<em id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complement</em>, and <em id="S2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inverse</em> are defined, respectively, by <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\mathrm{r}}_{i}=\pi_{n+1-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\mathrm{r}}_{i}=\pi_{n+1-i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\mathrm{c}}_{i}=n+1-\pi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\mathrm{c}}_{i}=n+1-\pi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}_{\pi_{i}}=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}_{\pi_{i}}=i</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Geometrically, these operations reflect the plot in a vertical line, reflect
it in a horizontal line, and reflect it in the main diagonal, respectively.
They act on mesh patterns by applying the same reflection to both the
underlying permutation and the shaded cells. In particular, if
<math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(\tau,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(\tau,R)</annotation></semantics></math> has length <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex10X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P^{\mathrm{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P^{\mathrm{r}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl(\tau^{\mathrm{r}},\{(k-a,b):(a,b)\in R\}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl(\tau^{\mathrm{r}},\{(k-a,b):(a,b)\in R\}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex10Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P^{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P^{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl(\tau^{\mathrm{c}},\{(a,k-b):(a,b)\in R\}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl(\tau^{\mathrm{c}},\{(a,k-b):(a,b)\in R\}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex10Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl(\tau^{-1},\{(b,a):(a,b)\in R\}\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl(\tau^{-1},\{(b,a):(a,b)\in R\}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">These symmetries preserve containment, avoidance, and coincidence. They also
preserve boxed patterns: <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)^{\mathrm{r}}=\operatorname{Box}(\tau^{\mathrm{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)^{\mathrm{r}}=\operatorname{Box}(\tau^{\mathrm{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)^{\mathrm{c}}=\operatorname{Box}(\tau^{\mathrm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)^{\mathrm{c}}=\operatorname{Box}(\tau^{\mathrm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)^{-1}=\operatorname{Box}(\tau^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)^{-1}=\operatorname{Box}(\tau^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq[k-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq[k-1]</annotation></semantics></math>, a <em id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vincular pattern</em> <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,X)</annotation></semantics></math> requires
<math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{x+1}=i_{x}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{x+1}=i_{x}+1</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>. We indicate bonds by underlining; thus
<math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{41}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{41}3</annotation></semantics></math> is the pattern for which the entries playing <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>
are adjacent. The notation <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent. A
<em id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bivincular pattern</em> <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> additionally requires that the selected
values of ranks <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y+1</annotation></semantics></math> be consecutive for each <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Vincular and
bivincular patterns are mesh patterns: position bonds shade complete columns,
and value bonds shade complete rows. For background on all of these families
see Kitaev <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and the survey of Steingrímsson
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For any pattern <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}(P)=\bigcup_{n\geq 0}\operatorname{Av}_{n}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Av</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}(P)=\bigcup_{n\geq 0}\operatorname{Av}_{n}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Two patterns <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are
<em id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coincident</em>, written <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}Q</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}_{n}(P)=\operatorname{Av}_{n}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}_{n}(P)=\operatorname{Av}_{n}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, every permutation contains <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it contains <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
In informal terms, the two pattern-avoidance tests are indistinguishable even
though the patterns may be drawn differently. This is stronger than
<em id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Wilf equivalence</em>, which asks only for
<math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(P)|=|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(Q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(P)|=|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(Q)|</annotation></semantics></math> in every length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We shall also use the obstruction to coincidence with classical patterns
introduced by Tenner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Write
<math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)=\{(i,\tau_{i}):1\leq i\leq k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)=\{(i,\tau_{i}):1\leq i\leq k\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the graph of
<math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. A shaded square <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\in R</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<em id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pointless square</em>, or an enclosed diagonal of length one, if its closed
square has no vertex in <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, a set of shaded squares</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{(a+i,b+i):0\leq i\leq c\}\subseteq R" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{(a+i,b+i):0\leq i\leq c\}\subseteq R</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">is an <em id="S2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">enclosed NE-diagonal</em> if</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)\cap\{(a+i,b+i):0\leq i\leq c+1\}=\{(a+i,b+i):1\leq i\leq c\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)\cap\{(a+i,b+i):0\leq i\leq c+1\}=\{(a+i,b+i):1\leq i\leq c\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly,
<math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{(a+i,b-i):0\leq i\leq c\}\subseteq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{(a+i,b-i):0\leq i\leq c\}\subseteq R</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<em id="S2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">enclosed SE-diagonal</em> if</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)\cap\{(a+i,b+1-i):0\leq i\leq c+1\}=\{(a+i,b+1-i):1\leq i\leq c\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)\cap\{(a+i,b+1-i):0\leq i\leq c+1\}=\{(a+i,b+1-i):1\leq i\leq c\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.4" class="ltx_p">Either type has length <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c+1</annotation></semantics></math> and is called <em id="S2.p8.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper</em> when its length is
greater than one. This is the square-based formulation in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Definition 3.1]</cite>. For a mesh pattern <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(\tau,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(\tau,R)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{enc}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>enc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{enc}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> denote its set
of enclosed diagonals, regarded as sets of shaded squares.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the mesh patterns <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(\tau,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(\tau,R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=(\sigma,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=(\sigma,S)</annotation></semantics></math> are coincident, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{enc}(P)=\operatorname{enc}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>enc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>enc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{enc}(P)=\operatorname{enc}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Equality of enclosed diagonals is necessary but is not sufficient for general
mesh-pattern coincidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We shall use the
necessary direction explicitly in the proof of
<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A mesh <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">superfluous</em> for <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> if its shading imposes no
additional avoidance condition, that is,
<math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,R)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}(\tau,\varnothing)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,R)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}(\tau,\varnothing)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus “superfluous” describes the whole
mesh; it does not mean that its cells can necessarily be removed independently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The mesh <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is superfluous for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{enc}((\tau,R))=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>enc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{enc}((\tau,R))=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
