<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.F1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.F1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(132)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>132</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(132)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.F1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(2413)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(2413)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.F1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(123)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>123</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(123)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Enclosed diagonals in boxed meshes. Enclosed diagonals are drawn
hatched and outlined as well as coloured, so that they remain distinguishable
in grayscale. The left-hand mesh has none; the marked central square in the
middle is a pointless enclosed diagonal; the two marked squares on the right
form a proper SE-diagonal of length two.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Thus boxed patterns are <em id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> generally free of enclosed diagonals.
Indeed, Tenner’s classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Corollary 3.8]</cite> says that the
only boxed patterns without one are those with underlying permutations
<math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,12,21,132,213,231," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>132</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>213</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>231</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,12,21,132,213,231,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="312" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>312</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">312</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, every boxed pattern of
length at least four has an enclosed diagonal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Boxed patterns as interval-consecutive patterns</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an integer interval <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> for the subword of <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>
obtained by retaining entries whose values lie in <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. An occurrence
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{i_{1}}\cdots\pi_{i_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i_{1}}\cdots\pi_{i_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">boxed occurrence</em> if
the open axis-parallel rectangle determined by its leftmost, rightmost, lowest
and highest selected points contains no further point of the plot of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding mesh pattern is denoted <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because a permutation plot has no repeated abscissae or ordinates, the
condition can equivalently be stated with the closed rectangle: the closed
bounding rectangle contains no plot point other than the selected ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and every permutation <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, the following are
equivalent.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some interval </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of values, the word </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|_{I}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a consecutive
factor whose standardization is </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose first that points with positions <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> form a boxed
occurrence, and let <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimum and maximum of their values.
No other point with value in <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[u,v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[u,v]</annotation></semantics></math> can have position strictly between
<math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the selected entries form a consecutive factor of
<math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|_{[u,v]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|_{[u,v]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose a consecutive factor of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> standardizes to <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimum and maximum values in this factor. Any extra
point in its bounding rectangle would have value in <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[u,v]\subseteq I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[u,v]\subseteq I</annotation></semantics></math> and
position between the first and last entries of the factor. It would therefore
appear inside the factor of <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The criterion places boxed avoidance beside simsun-type restrictions, but with
all value intervals rather than only initial intervals. It also gives a uniform
way to search for occurrences: restrict to intervals, then use consecutive
pattern matching. A second equivalent formulation, used in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, is obtained by repeatedly deleting the leftmost, rightmost,
smallest or largest entry: <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if some such
sequence of external deletions ends at <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deciding whether a given permutation contains a given boxed pattern has also
been studied algorithmically. Bruner and Lackner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>
devote a section to boxed mesh patterns and give an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm; Cho,
Na and Sim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> improve this to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{2}m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{2}m)</annotation></semantics></math> and then to
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{2}\log m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{2}\log m)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the pattern; and Amit, Bille, Hagge Cording, Li Gørtz and Wedel
Vildhøj <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> give an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm, which is optimal
because the output can have size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The interval formulation also gives a useful inheritance principle. Say that
<math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\mathfrak{S}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\mathfrak{S}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interval factor</em> of
<math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau=\tau_{1}\cdots\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\tau_{1}\cdots\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> if, for some <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau_{j},\tau_{j+1},\ldots,\tau_{j+r-1}\}=\{a,a+1,\ldots,a+r-1\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau_{j},\tau_{j+1},\ldots,\tau_{j+r-1}\}=\{a,a+1,\ldots,a+r-1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma=\operatorname{st}(\tau_{j}\cdots\tau_{j+r-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>st</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\operatorname{st}(\tau_{j}\cdots\tau_{j+r-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the entries forming
<math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> occupy consecutive positions and consecutive ranks in <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is an interval factor of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, then every occurrence of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> contains an occurrence of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\sigma))\subseteq\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\sigma))\subseteq\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, failure of the Stanley–Wilf property for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> implies
failure of the property for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take the selected points of a boxed <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> occurrence that correspond to the
factor <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau_{j}\cdots\tau_{j+r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}\cdots\tau_{j+r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. No other selected point lies horizontally
between the first and last of these points, because the positions of the
factor are consecutive. No other selected point lies vertically between
their lowest and highest points, because their ranks are consecutive. An
unselected point in the smaller bounding rectangle would also lie in the
bounding rectangle of the original boxed occurrence. Hence the selected
factor is a boxed <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> occurrence. Taking contrapositives gives the class
inclusion, and the final assertion follows by comparing cardinalities.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For example, Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3 Boxed patterns as interval-consecutive patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and the doubled-letter construction
show immediately that <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> has factorially many avoiders whenever
<math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> has an interval factor <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="123" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>123</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">123</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="321" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>321</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">321</annotation></semantics></math>. The “good pattern” argument
of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> handles many patterns without such a monotone interval
factor and is therefore genuinely stronger than this inheritance principle.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The exceptional length-four patterns</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reverse, complement and inverse preserve boxed containment and avoidance. A
direct calculation gives the two orbits in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>); in particular,
inverse fixes <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2143" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2143</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2143</annotation></semantics></math> and interchanges <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2413" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2413</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2413</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3142" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3142</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3142</annotation></semantics></math>. The distinction is
enumerative as well as formal:</p>
<table id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">0</th>
<th id="S4.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">1</th>
<th id="S4.p1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">2</th>
<th id="S4.p1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">3</th>
<th id="S4.p1.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">4</th>
<th id="S4.p1.2.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">5</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2143))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2143</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2143))|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">1</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">1</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">2</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">6</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">23</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">106</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.p1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2413))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2413))|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">1</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">1</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">2</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">6</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">23</td>
<td id="S4.p1.2.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">104</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that an occurrence of the vincular pattern <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{41}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{41}3</annotation></semantics></math> is a
subsequence <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> in that positional order, with <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent and <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A permutation contains the boxed pattern <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(2413)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(2413)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it contains
the vincular pattern <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{41}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{41}3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> is a boxed <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2413" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2413</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2413</annotation></semantics></math> occurrence, so
<math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. Read the entries between <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the
rectangle is empty, each is either above <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> or below <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Beginning with <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>
and ending with <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an adjacent transition <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime},c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime},c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from the upper to
the lower region. Since <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}\geq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}\geq b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> gives
<math id="S4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> lie weakly between <math id="S4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in position, so
<math id="S4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> precedes <math id="S4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> follows <math id="S4.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b^{\prime},c^{\prime},d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b^{\prime},c^{\prime},d</annotation></semantics></math> is an occurrence of
<math id="S4.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{41}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{41}3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the converse, use the external-deletion characterization in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Boxed patterns as interval-consecutive patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. It is enough to show that whenever a
permutation of length greater than four contains <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{41}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{41}3</annotation></semantics></math>, one of
its four external entries can be deleted while preserving such an occurrence.
Choose an occurrence <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math>. If an external entry is not among these four,
delete it; the bond survives, since <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent and therefore no
entry lies between them. The only remaining case is therefore that <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math>
are respectively the leftmost, largest, smallest and rightmost entries of the
whole permutation; the assignment of roles is forced, because by
<math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> the largest of the four is <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and the smallest is <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>,
while in positional order the first is <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and the last is <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be the entry immediately preceding <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, deleting the largest entry <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent to <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,x,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,x,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> is still an occurrence. Suppose instead that <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq a</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;x&lt;d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;x&lt;d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> is an occurrence and the leftmost entry <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> may be deleted. The only remaining possibility is <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=a</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is the first entry and <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent, the permutation begins with <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the last entry, so, because the permutation has length greater than four, the entry <math id="S4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> immediately following <math id="S4.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> exists and satisfies <math id="S4.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\neq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\neq d</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are respectively the smallest and largest entries of the permutation, <math id="S4.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;y&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;y&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;a&lt;y&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;a&lt;y&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,y</annotation></semantics></math> is an occurrence and <math id="S4.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> may be deleted. If <math id="S4.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>; deleting the smallest entry <math id="S4.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S4.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent, and <math id="S4.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,y,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,y,d</annotation></semantics></math> is an occurrence. Thus an external entry can always be deleted while preserving an occurrence. Repeating the argument ends at the permutation <math id="S4.p5.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2413" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2413</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2413</annotation></semantics></math>, proving boxed containment.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contrary to what one might first expect, boxed meshes are not generally free of
enclosed diagonals; see Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The two meshes in
Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> have the same nonempty set of enclosed diagonals: the
pointless square <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the enclosed-diagonal invariant of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is consistent with the coincidence, but
Tenner’s superfluous-mesh theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> does not prove it: that
theorem applies precisely when there are no enclosed diagonals. Nor are the
two meshes nested, so the one-square Shading Lemma
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Lemma 3.11]</cite> cannot be applied directly. Its
simultaneous extension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Section 7]</cite> is also a
sufficient local shading criterion, not a converse that would establish this
coincidence. Finally, the coincidence theorem for two vincular patterns does
not apply because the boxed mesh is not vincular. The tightening argument
above is therefore not an immediate consequence of these earlier results; it
supplies the required global movement of the adjacency bond.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2413))|=|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(3142))|=\mathsf{SB}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3142</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2413))|=|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(3142))|=\mathsf{SB}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SB}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SB}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th semi-Baxter number (OEIS A117106). With
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SB}_{0}=\mathsf{SB}_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SB}_{0}=\mathsf{SB}_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, these numbers satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SB}_{n}=\frac{11n^{2}+11n-6}{(n+4)(n+3)}\mathsf{SB}_{n-1}+\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}{(n+4)(n+3)}\mathsf{SB}_{n-2}\qquad(n\geq 2)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SB}_{n}=\frac{11n^{2}+11n-6}{(n+4)(n+3)}\mathsf{SB}_{n-1}+\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}{(n+4)(n+3)}\mathsf{SB}_{n-2}\qquad(n\geq 2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and, for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SB}_{n}=\frac{24}{(n-1)n^{2}(n+1)(n+2)}\sum_{j=0}^{n}\binom{n}{j+2}\binom{n+2}{j}\binom{n+j+2}{j+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SB}_{n}=\frac{24}{(n-1)n^{2}(n+1)(n+2)}\sum_{j=0}^{n}\binom{n}{j+2}\binom{n+2}{j}\binom{n+j+2}{j+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, the avoiders of <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(2413)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(2413)</annotation></semantics></math> are the
semi-Baxter permutations, and hence have cardinality <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SB}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SB}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Inverse symmetry gives the same enumeration for <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(3142)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3142</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(3142)</annotation></semantics></math>. The recurrence and sum are the semi-Baxter enumeration of
Bouvel et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Proposition 13 and Theorem 14]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SB}_{n}\sim A\mu^{n}n^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲𝖡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SB}_{n}\sim A\mu^{n}n^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=((\sqrt{5}-1)/2)^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=((\sqrt{5}-1)/2)^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and an explicit positive constant <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Boxed <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2413" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2413</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2413</annotation></semantics></math> therefore satisfies the Stanley–Wilf
property. It also follows that no injection from <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math>
to <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2413))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(2413))</annotation></semantics></math> can exist for every <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>: such an injection would force a
factorial lower bound on an exponentially bounded sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Combining <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> with the factorial constructions in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> leaves exactly one unresolved dihedral orbit at length four,
namely <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2143,3412\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2143</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3412</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2143,3412\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The combination is worth recording as a single global statement. It also
clarifies that coincidence and growth are separate questions: the
noncoincidence theorem in <a href="#S5" title="5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is not needed for the
factorial lower bound in large lengths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If
</span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\notin\{2143,3412,2413,3142\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2143</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3412</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3142</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\notin\{2143,3412,2413,3142\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))|\geq\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Av}_{n}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))|\geq\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor!</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
so </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> does not have the Stanley–Wilf property.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{2413,3142\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2413</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3142</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{2413,3142\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the Stanley–Wilf
property and is enumerated by the semi-Baxter numbers.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Stanley–Wilf property remains open for
</span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{2143,3412\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2143</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3412</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{2143,3412\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; these two cases are equivalent by complement.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, no boxed pattern of length at least five has the
Stanley–Wilf property.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Part (i) is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Theorem 3]</cite>. Stirling’s formula gives <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor!\right)^{1/n}\sim\sqrt{\frac{n}{2e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\left\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\right\rfloor!\right)^{1/n}\sim\sqrt{\frac{n}{2e}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which tends to infinity. Part (ii) is
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, and part (iii) follows from complement
symmetry and the fact that neither of the preceding results applies to this
orbit. For <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> none of the four exceptional permutations in
part (i) has the required length, proving the last assertion.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Together with the short cases, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> gives an almost
complete classification over all boxed patterns. The Stanley–Wilf property
is known to hold for the boxed patterns with underlying permutations <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,12,21,132,213,231,312,2413,3142" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>132</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>213</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>231</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>312</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3142</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,12,21,132,213,231,312,2413,3142</annotation></semantics></math>;
it is known to fail for every other boxed pattern except <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2143" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2143</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2143</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3412" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3412</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3412</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first record the classical case, which is now a consequence of the general
coincidence theory for mesh patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathfrak{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is coincident with a classical
pattern if and only if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{132,213,231,312\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>132,213,231,312</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{132,213,231,312\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In every case the classical pattern is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> itself. In particular, no boxed
pattern of length at least four is coincident with a classical pattern.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 ([], Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, a coincident classical pattern would
have to be <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> itself. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 ([]). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, coincidence with
<math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> holds exactly when the full internal box has no enclosed diagonal.
Applying that criterion gives precisely the displayed list; this is also
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Proposition 1]</cite> and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Corollary 3.8]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The answer changes for vincular patterns, but only in small lengths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a vincular pattern of length <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> with at least one bond. Then
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}V</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the pair occurs in the following list:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=2:\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=2:\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(12)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{12}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(12)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{12},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(21)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{21};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(21)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{21};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=3:\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=3:\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(132)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{13}2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>132</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(132)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{13}2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(213)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{13}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>213</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(213)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{13},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(231)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{31}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>231</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>31</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(231)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{31},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(312)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{31}2;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>312</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>31</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(312)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{31}2;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=4:\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=4:\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(2413)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{41}3," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(2413)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{41}3,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(3142)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}3\underline{14}2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3142</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Box}(3142)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}3\underline{14}2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, there is no boxed–vincular coincidence in length <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=(\sigma,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=(\sigma,X)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}V</annotation></semantics></math>, the first necessary condition
in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 ([], Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> gives <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, while the second
gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{enc}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))=\operatorname{enc}(V)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>enc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>enc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{enc}(\operatorname{Box}(\tau))=\operatorname{enc}(V).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">We use this equality repeatedly.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix a bond <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in X</annotation></semantics></math>. It shades the complete column <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, including the
two boundary cells <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, neither of which is shaded in the boxed
mesh. If <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,0)</annotation></semantics></math> touched no point of <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, it would be a pointless
enclosed diagonal of <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> that is absent from <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting
(<a href="#S5.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). It must therefore touch one of
<math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c+1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c+1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, so one of <math id="S5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c},\tau_{c+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c},\tau_{c+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the
same argument to <math id="S5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,k)</annotation></semantics></math> shows that one of them is <math id="S5.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau_{c},\tau_{c+1}\}=\{1,k\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau_{c},\tau_{c+1}\}=\{1,k\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the values <math id="S5.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> occur only once, two distinct bonds cannot both
satisfy (<a href="#S5.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>); consequently <math id="S5.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{c\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Applying complement to both patterns if necessary, we may assume that <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c+1}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c+1}=k</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> shades only column <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Every internal cell outside
column <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> must therefore touch a point of <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise it would
be a pointless enclosed diagonal of <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> that is absent from <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>,
contradicting (<a href="#S5.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose first that <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\leq k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\leq k-2</annotation></semantics></math>, so that column <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c+1</annotation></semantics></math> is an internal column.
Its <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> internal cells can be touched only by the two plot points <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c+1,\tau_{c+1})=(c+1,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c+1,\tau_{c+1})=(c+1,k)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c+2,\tau_{c+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c+2,\tau_{c+2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The first point touches only one internal cell of column <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c+1</annotation></semantics></math>, while the
second touches at most two. Thus at most three of the <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> internal cells
in this column can touch <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, at least one of these
cells is pointless, a contradiction. Hence <math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=k-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, if <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the internal column <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Its cells can
be touched only by <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c-1,\tau_{c-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c-1,\tau_{c-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,\tau_{c})=(c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,\tau_{c})=(c,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, the extreme-valued point touches only one internal cell and the other
point touches at most two. For <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, this leaves a pointless cell in
column <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have obtained both <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=k-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math>, which would force <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
This contradicts <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore no boxed–vincular coincidence can
occur in length at least five.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to settle <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, condition (<a href="#S5.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) leaves only
<math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{12}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{21}</annotation></semantics></math>. A permutation contains a classical
<math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it has an adjacent ascent: a permutation with no adjacent
ascent is decreasing. Hence <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly
<math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}\underline{21}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining these observations with
<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> gives the two asserted boxed coincidences.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S5.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) leaves exactly the four vincular
patterns displayed in the theorem. In Claesson’s generalized-pattern
notation, Lemma 21 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> proves <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b\text{--}a\text{--}c)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}(b\text{--}ac)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b\text{--}a\text{--}c)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}(b\text{--}ac)</annotation></semantics></math>,
that is, <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="213\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{13}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>213</mn><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">213\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{13}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Reverse and complement give the other three identities. Each of the four
underlying classical patterns is coincident with its boxed version by
<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, proving all four length-three cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally let <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Equation (<a href="#S5.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) gives twelve candidates.
The following six are impossible; the indicated cell is pointless and shaded
in the boxed mesh, but is not shaded in the vincular mesh, contradicting
(<a href="#S5.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</span></p>
<table id="S5.p14.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.p14.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p14.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.p14.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">vincular pattern</span></th>
<th id="S5.p14.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.p14.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">cell</span></th>
<th id="S5.p14.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.p14.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">vincular pattern</span></th>
<th id="S5.p14.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.p14.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">cell</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.p14.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p14.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{14}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn mathsize="0.900em">14</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{14}32</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.p14.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p14.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></th>
<td id="S5.p14.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{41}23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn mathsize="0.900em">41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{41}23</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.p14.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.p14.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p14.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{14}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn mathsize="0.900em">14</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{14}3</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.p14.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S5.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p14.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></th>
<td id="S5.p14.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\underline{41}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn mathsize="0.900em">41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\underline{41}2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.p14.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.p14.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.p14.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23\underline{41}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">23</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn mathsize="0.900em">41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23\underline{41}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.p14.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p14.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></th>
<td id="S5.p14.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\underline{14}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn mathsize="0.900em">14</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\underline{14}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.p14.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p14.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.3.1" class="ltx_text">The six candidates not eliminated in this way are <math id="S5.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{14}23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{14}23</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23\underline{14}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23\underline{14}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{41}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{41}3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\underline{14}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\underline{14}2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\underline{41}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\underline{41}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{41}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{41}32</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">The remaining four noncoincidences have the following explicit certificates.
In the last column we list every occurrence of the underlying classical
pattern in the separator; the entry following the semicolon is the omitted
fifth entry, which lies in its bounding rectangle and therefore blocks it from
being boxed.</span></p>
<table id="S5.p15.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.p15.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
