<p id="S5.p15.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p15.3.1" class="ltx_text">Thus each separator contains the indicated vincular pattern and avoids the
corresponding boxed pattern. The third candidate is the coincidence proved
in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, and the fourth is its reversal. This completes the
classification.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">There is also a natural coincidence with a genuinely bivincular pattern. It
does not merely repackage the mesh as a set of position bonds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(2413)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{41}3\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}(2413,\{2\},\{2\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2413</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(2413)\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}2\underline{41}3\mathrel{\equiv_{\rm c}}(2413,\{2\},\{2\})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 The exceptional length-four patterns ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, it is enough to show that among occurrences <math id="S5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S5.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\underline{41}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mn>41</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\underline{41}3</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;a&lt;d&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent) there is one satisfying <math id="S5.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=a+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose such an occurrence
<math id="S5.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> minimizing <math id="S5.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-a</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;e&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;e&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math> for some entry <math id="S5.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of the permutation,
then, because <math id="S5.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent, <math id="S5.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> lies either to the left of <math id="S5.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> or
to the right of <math id="S5.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. In the first case <math id="S5.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> is a new occurrence with a
smaller gap; in the second, <math id="S5.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,e</annotation></semantics></math> is. Both contradict minimality. Hence
<math id="S5.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=a+1</annotation></semantics></math>. The reverse implication is immediate.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p">The three coincident meshes in Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 5.3. ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> are shown in
Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5 Classical, vincular and bivincular coincidences ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The last has both a complete shaded column and a
complete shaded row, so it is genuinely bivincular.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
