}{
}{
}{
}{}}{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 0.07 0.07 M 0.07 7.17 L 21.42 7.17 M 0.07 14.33 L 21.42 14.33 M 7.17 0.07 L 7.17 21.42 M 14.33 0.07 L 14.33 21.42 M 21.42 21.42}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
{}{{}}{}{{{}}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{}{}{}{}{}}{}\lxSVG@fillstroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 7.17 7.17 M 8.68 7.17 C 8.68 8 8 8.68 7.17 8.68 C 6.33 8.68 5.65 8 5.65 7.17 C 5.65 6.33 6.33 5.65 7.17 5.65 C 8 5.65 8.68 6.33 8.68 7.17 Z M 7.17 7.17}{} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
{}{{}}{}{{{}}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{}{}{}{}{}}{}\lxSVG@fillstroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 14.33 14.33 M 15.84 14.33 C 15.84 15.16 15.16 15.84 14.33 15.84 C 13.5 15.84 12.82 15.16 12.82 14.33 C 12.82 13.5 13.5 12.82 14.33 12.82 C 15.16 12.82 15.84 13.5 15.84 14.33 Z M 14.33 14.33}{} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
\lxSVG@closescope {{
{}{}{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The avoidance sequence of this pattern is immediate: for each <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the
decreasing permutation <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n(n-1)\cdots 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(n-1)\cdots 1</annotation></semantics></math> is its unique avoider. However, to our
knowledge no general formula for its distribution is known, either in the
mesh-pattern literature or in the literature on the strong Bruhat order,
where only the extremal values and the mean of the statistic appear; see
Corollary <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 8.3. ‣ 8 Boxed 12 and strong Bruhat covers ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a>. The statistic has a natural
interpretation in a different area of algebraic combinatorics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ell(\pi)=|\{(i,j):1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,\ \pi_{i}&gt;\pi_{j}\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\pi)=|\{(i,j):1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,\ \pi_{i}&gt;\pi_{j}\}|</annotation></semantics></math> be its inversion number. If <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(i\leftrightarrow j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(i\leftrightarrow j)</annotation></semantics></math>
be obtained by interchanging the entries in positions <math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The
<em id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strong Bruhat order</em> on <math id="S8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the transitive closure of the
covering relations</p>
<table id="S8.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi\lessdot\pi(i\leftrightarrow j)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\ell\bigl(\pi(i\leftrightarrow j)\bigr)=\ell(\pi)+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\lessdot\pi(i\leftrightarrow j)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\ell\bigl(\pi(i\leftrightarrow j)\bigr)=\ell(\pi)+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S8.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">up-degree</em> of <math id="S8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{+}(\pi)=|\{\sigma\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}:\pi\lessdot\sigma\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{+}(\pi)=|\{\sigma\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}:\pi\lessdot\sigma\}|</annotation></semantics></math>;
the down-degree <math id="S8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{-}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{-}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined analogously by counting
<math id="S8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\lessdot\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\lessdot\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. See Adin and Roichman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> for these
statistics and Bouvel, Ferrari and Tenner
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for descriptions of Bruhat covering relations
in mesh-pattern language.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of occurrences of <math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(12)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8.1</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every permutation <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\pi)=d_{+}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\pi)=d_{+}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choose <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi_{i}&lt;\pi_{j}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi_{i}&lt;\pi_{j}=b</annotation></semantics></math>. Interchanging <math id="S8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> changes the
inversion number by <math id="S8.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1+2|\{k:i&lt;k&lt;j,\ a&lt;\pi_{k}&lt;b\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2|\{k:i&lt;k&lt;j,\ a&lt;\pi_{k}&lt;b\}|</annotation></semantics></math>. It therefore gives an upward Bruhat cover exactly when no entry strictly
between positions <math id="S8.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> has value strictly between <math id="S8.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. This is
precisely the empty-cell condition for the selected pair to be an occurrence
of <math id="S8.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Box}(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Box</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Box}(12)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8.2</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kitaev and Liese <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorem 12]</cite> proved that <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> has
Catalan distribution on <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}_{n}(132)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>132</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}_{n}(132)</annotation></semantics></math>: for <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq k\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq k\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S8.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl|\{\pi\in\operatorname{Av}_{n}(132):b(\pi)=k\}\bigr|=C(n-1,k)=\frac{n-k}{n}\binom{n-1+k}{n-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>132</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl|\{\pi\in\operatorname{Av}_{n}(132):b(\pi)=k\}\bigr|=C(n-1,k)=\frac{n-k}{n}\binom{n-1+k}{n-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(r,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(r,k)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,k)</annotation></semantics></math> entry of Catalan’s triangle. In view of
<a href="#S8.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 8.1. ‣ 8 Boxed 12 and strong Bruhat covers ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>, this also gives the distribution of the strong
Bruhat up-degree on the Catalan class <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Av}_{n}(132)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Av</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>132</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Av}_{n}(132)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the notation of (<a href="#S6.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ 6.3 Average occurrence counts for boxed patterns ‣ 6 The boxed mesh pattern 123 ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), define its
distribution polynomial by</p>
<table id="S8.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}(q)=F_{n,12}(q)=\sum_{\pi\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}}q^{b(\pi)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}(q)=F_{n,12}(q)=\sum_{\pi\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}}q^{b(\pi)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Complementation interchanges up-degree and down-degree. Consequently the
results of Adin and Roichman
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Propositions 2.1 and 2.9, and Theorem 4.1]</cite>
translate as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 8.3</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S8.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\deg D_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\deg D_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{4}\right\rfloor," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{4}\right\rfloor,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[q^{\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor}]D_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[q^{\lfloor n^{2}/4\rfloor}]D_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}n,&amp;n\text{ odd},\\
n/2,&amp;n\text{ even},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> odd</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> even</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}n,&amp;n\text{ odd},\\
n/2,&amp;n\text{ even},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{D_{n}^{\prime}(1)}{n!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{D_{n}^{\prime}(1)}{n!}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(n+1)H_{n}-2n," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(n+1)H_{n}-2n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}1/j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}=\sum_{j=1}^{n}1/j</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th harmonic number.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8.4</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The familiar stronger algebraic properties do not hold in general. Already</p>
<table id="S8.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{3}(q)=1+2q+3q^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{3}(q)=1+2q+3q^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">has nonreal zeros. Moreover, for every <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> the constant coefficient is
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas (<a href="#S8.E27" title="Equation 27 ‣ Corollary 8.3. ‣ 8 Boxed 12 and strong Bruhat covers ‣ Coincidences and Growth of Boxed Mesh Patterns" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) shows that the leading coefficient is
greater than <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is not palindromic, ruling out
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-positivity in its usual palindromic sense. Log-concavity also fails:
the polynomial <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{7}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{7}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> recorded in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> has <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[q^{10}]D_{7}(q)=129" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>129</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[q^{10}]D_{7}(q)=129</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[q^{11}]D_{7}(q)=26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[q^{11}]D_{7}(q)=26</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[q^{12}]D_{7}(q)=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>12</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[q^{12}]D_{7}(q)=7</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26^{2}&lt;129\cdot 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>26</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>129</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26^{2}&lt;129\cdot 7</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, exhaustive enumeration gives a
unimodal coefficient sequence for every <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This evidence suggests that unimodality may be the appropriate surviving
property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 8.5</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficient sequence of <math id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodal.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8.6</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, harmonic numbers also describe the complete distribution of a
rather different mesh pattern. Let</p>
<table id="S8.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
